# README --- infix README  -*- coding: utf-8; mode: org; -*-

#+title: infix

*This work still in progress*.

~infix~ is a small reusable Nix layer.

* Getting Started

Add ~infix~ to the consuming flake.  If the project already has a
~nixpkgs~ input, make ~infix~ follow it so both flakes evaluate against
the same package set.

#+begin_src nix
  {
    inputs = {
      nixpkgs.url = "git+https://github.com/NixOS/nixpkgs.git?ref=nixpkgs-unstable";
      infix.url = "git+https://codeberg.org/bingshan/infix.git";
      infix.inputs.nixpkgs.follows = "nixpkgs";
    };
  }
#+end_src

** Use the Package Overlays

Use ~overlays.default~ when the consuming project wants packages from
this repository under ~pkgs~.

#+begin_src nix
  pkgs = import nixpkgs {
    inherit
      system
      ;

    overlays = [
      infix.overlays.default
    ];
  };
#+end_src

The default overlay is built from ~src/packages~.

Use ~overlays.emacs-packages~ when the consuming project wants the Emacs
package scope customizations from this repository.

#+begin_src nix
  pkgs = import nixpkgs {
    inherit
      system
      ;

    overlays = [
      infix.overlays.emacs-packages
    ];
  };
#+end_src

The Emacs overlay extends ~emacsPackagesFor~ with manually packaged
Emacs Lisp packages and a few MELPA package overrides.

** Use the flake-parts Modules

The exported ~flakeModules~ are intended for flake-parts users.  Import
only the modules needed by the consuming flake.

#+begin_src nix
  outputs =
    inputs@{
      flake-parts,
      infix,
      ...
    }:
    flake-parts.lib.mkFlake
      {
        inherit
          inputs
          ;
      }
      {
        imports = [
          infix.flakeModules.nixosConfigurations
        ];

        systems = [
          "x86_64-linux"
        ];
      };
#+end_src

